map \(g:S^6\to X\) satisfying \(g_*[S^6]=[X]\), and this particular map
is a homotopy equivalence.
\end{theorem}

II:4.2.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[Refuted surface assertion, with explicit example]\label{cdp:st:C.3.3}

The assertion that a secondary Hopf surface has torsion in
$H_1(-;\Z)$ is false. There is a secondary Hopf surface $Q_H$ with
$H_1(Q_H;\Z)=\Z$ and $H^2(Q_H;\Z)=0$.
\end{proposition}

II:4.4.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[Proved exclusion of bielliptic fibres]\label{cdp:st:C.3.7}

A compact complex threefold $Y$ with $b_1(Y)=b_2(Y)=0$ has no
proper connected-fibre holomorphic map to a compact curve whose
general fibre is bielliptic.
\end{proposition}

II:4.5.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[Proved exclusion of elliptic Hopf general fibres]\label{cdp:st:C.3.8}

The very general Hopf fibre of the algebraic reduction in \ref{cdp:st:C.3.1}
has algebraic dimension zero. In particular neither primary nor
secondary Hopf surfaces of algebraic dimension one can be its
general fibre. This exclusion uses no restriction-surjectivity
assertion from CDP98 or CDP20.
\end{proposition}

II:5.1.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[The conormal filtration and its exact section criterion]\label{cdp:st:A.1.1}\label{wbII:prop:criterion}\label{wbII:prop:filtration}\label{wbII:lem:conormal}\label{wbII:lem:conormal-injective}\label{wbII:prop:missing-global-section-criterion}\label{wbII:prop:missing-cotangent-filtration}
The conormal map $d:N^*\to V$, defined by $[F]\mapsto dF|_S$ for a local defining equation $F$, is injective. With $K=\ker(V\to Q)$, there are canonical exact sequences
\begin{align}
0&\longrightarrow N^*\xrightarrow{d}K\longrightarrow T\longrightarrow0,
\label{cdp:A:filtration1}\\
0&\longrightarrow K\longrightarrow V\longrightarrow Q\longrightarrow0.
\label{cdp:A:filtration2}
\end{align}

II:5.10.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[The hypotheses satisfied by the compact example]\label{cdp:st:B3}
The threefold $Y$ of Statement \ref{cdp:st:B2} has $H^1(Y,\Z)=0$, $H^2(Y,\Z)\simeq\Z^{11}$, algebraic dimension $3$, and $e(Y)=24$. Its map $g$ is holomorphic, proper, surjective, and has connected fibres. Thus it tests the fibrewise inference in Section 7 of \cite{CDP20}; it does not satisfy the vanishing of $H^2(Y,\Z)$ or algebraic dimension one required by that paper's Theorem 2.2.
\end{proposition}

II:5.12.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[Counterexample: Vanishing on the support alone does not imply vanishing on a multiple fibre]\label{cdp:st:A.3.2}
There is a compact smooth complex threefold $g:Y\to\PP^1$ with connected general fibre an abelian surface and a smooth fibre support $S$ of multiplicity $m$, for every $m\ge3$, and a line bundle $B$ on $Y$, such that
\[
H^0(S,(\Omega_Y^1\otimes B)|_S)=0,
\qquad h^0(mS,(\Omega_Y^1\otimes B)|_{mS})=3.
\tag{A.12}\label{cdp:eq:A.12}
\]
Its conormal has exact order $m$. The replacement is (A.10)--(A.11), with the graded twists retained.
\end{proposition}

II:5.13.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[The precise finite-translation step in Remarks 7.2 and 7.4]\label{cdp:st:A.3.3}
For a fixed multiple fibre $mS$, suppose that the set of line bundles $B$ for which $H^0(S,(\Omega_Y^1\otimes B)|_S)=0$ contains a dense open subset $U$ of the relevant Picard component. The intersection
\[
\bigcap_{j=0}^{m-1}\{B:B\otimes\OO_Y(-jS)\in U\}
\tag{A.14}\label{cdp:eq:A.14}
\]
is a dense open subset on which the full-fibre ambient group vanishes, whenever the displayed translations preserve that component. The same assertion for a finite union of components follows by using the appropriate dense open subset in each translated component.
\end{proposition}

II:5.14.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[The smooth-multiple assertion of Remark 7.2]\label{cdp:st:A.3.4}
Under the hypotheses of CDP20 Proposition~7.3, let $mS$ be a full fibre whose reduced support $S$ is smooth. For every real irrational transgression parameter $r$ of \ref{cdp:st:D.1.9},
\[
 H^0\bigl(mS,(\Omega_Y^1\otimes L_r)|_{mS}\bigr)=0.
 \tag{A.15a}\label{cdp:eq:A.15a}
\]
The same simultaneous vanishing on all such fibres holds on the complement of a proper complex analytic subset of $\Pic(Y)$. The assertion includes the entire scheme-theoretic fibre, with every nilpotent layer.
\end{proposition}

II:5.16.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[Both fibre groups on the specified $W$]\label{cdp:st:A.4.1}
For the reduced normal-crossings fibre $W$ with normalization $\eta:\operatorname{dP}_6\to W$ in the specified threefold $X$, and every $L\in\Pic(X)$, put $A=(K_X\otimes L^{-1})|_W$. Then
\[
H^0(W,Q\otimes A)=0,
\qquad H^0(W,\Omega_X^1|_W\otimes A)\simeq\C.
\tag{A.15}\label{cdp:eq:A.15}
\]
The same two conclusions hold with $A=B|_W$ for every $B\in\Pic(X)$.
\end{proposition}

II:5.17.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[Counterexample: A specific implication in the printed proof of Proposition 7.3]\label{cdp:st:A.4.2}
The implication that a ruled minimal surface $S_0$ with a one-parameter family of numerically trivial line bundles $M_t$ and nonzero sections of $\Omega^1_{S_0}\otimes M_t$ must be ruled over a curve of genus at least two is false when the family $M_t$ is allowed to be constant, as the printed proof explicitly allows.
\end{proposition}

II:5.18.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[The omitted genus-one case for the standard rank-one fibre]\label{cdp:st:A.4.3}
Let $E$ be an elliptic curve, let $P\in\Pic(E)$, and let $T=\PP_E(\OO_E\oplus P)$ with its zero and infinity sections $D_0,D_\infty$. Glue $D_0$ to $D_\infty$ by a translation of $E$ to form the compact normal-crossings surface $S$, and denote its normalization by $\eta:T\to S$. Every line bundle $A$ on $S$ whose pullback $\eta^*A$ is topologically trivial satisfies
\[
H^0(S,\widetilde\Omega_S^1\otimes A)=
\begin{cases}\C,&A\simeq\OO_S,\\0,&A\not\simeq\OO_S.\end{cases}
\tag{A.16}\label{cdp:eq:A.16}
\]
\end{proposition}

II:5.19.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[Canonical triviality on an irreducible reduced torus fibre]\label{cdp:st:A.4.4}
Let $g:Y\to C$ be a proper holomorphic map from a connected smooth compact threefold onto a smooth compact curve, with connected fibres and general fibre a complex two-torus. Every irreducible reduced full fibre $S=g^{-1}(c)$ has $\omega_S\simeq\OO_S$. When $H^2(Y,\Q)=0$, it therefore satisfies $h^q(S,\OO_S)=(1,2,1)$.
\end{proposition}

II:5.21.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[Euler numbers of these irreducible normal-crossings fibres]\label{cdp:st:A.4.6}
For $S$ in \ref{cdp:st:A.4.5},
\[
e(S)=\begin{cases}
2,&\widetilde S\text{ is rational},\\
0,&\widetilde S\text{ is elliptic ruled or Hopf}.
\end{cases}
\tag{A.18}\label{cdp:eq:A.18}
\]
Thus an arbitrary compact torus fibration whose fibres are irreducible reduced normal-crossings surfaces or multiples of smooth surfaces of Euler number zero has
\[
c_3(Y)=e(Y)=2r,
\tag{A.19}\label{cdp:eq:A.19}
\]
where $r$ is the number of rational-normalization fibres. For $b_1(Y)=b_2(Y)=0$, one has $r\in\{0,1\}$ and $b_3(Y)=2-2r$.
\end{proposition}

II:5.22.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[Unitary-flat differentials on every compact reduced complex surface]\label{cdp:st:A.4.7}
Let $S$ be a compact reduced complex surface, let $\mathbb A$ be a rank-one unitary complex local system on its underlying topological space, and let $A=\mathbb A\otimes_{\C}\OO_S$ be its associated holomorphic line bundle. There is a canonical complex-linear injection
\[
 H^0(S,\widetilde\Omega_S^1\otimes A)
       \longrightarrow H^1(S,\mathbb A).
 \tag{A.20}\label{cdp:eq:A.20}
\]
The construction uses continuous local primitives that are holomorphic on the normalization; it does not assert that those primitives are holomorphic on a non-seminormal surface.
\end{proposition}

II:6.1.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[What trivial normalization pullback forces]\label{cdp:st:A.5.1}
Let $S$ be a connected reduced full fibre of a proper map from a complex manifold to a smooth curve. For every line bundle $A$ on $S$ with $\eta^*A\simeq\OO_{\widetilde S}$, the ambient group contains the canonical image of $H^0(\widetilde S,\OO_{\widetilde S})$. In particular it is nonzero. This applies to the subcase of Proposition~7.3 in which all $\eta^*(L_t|_S)$ are trivial. It is compatible with the vanishing of $H^0(S,Q\otimes A)$ through the exact maps of \ref{cdp:st:A.1.1}, as \ref{cdp:st:A.4.1} demonstrates.
\end{proposition}

II:6.2.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[Normal Gorenstein surfaces with vanishing first Chern class and Euler characteristic]\label{cdp:st:A.5.2}
A compact connected normal Gorenstein complex surface $S$ with $c_1(\omega_S)=0$ in $H^2(S,\Q)$ and $\chi(S,\OO_S)=0$ is smooth. Consequently every singular irreducible reduced divisor in a compact complex threefold $Y$ with $H^2(Y,\Q)=0$ is nonnormal. In particular the assertion of CDP20 Proposition~4.11 holds without its monodromy-dependent restrictions on the smooth fibre.
\end{proposition}

II:7.1.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[Every torus-degeneration fibre has even Euler number]\label{cdp:st:C.2.UN.1}
Let $f:Y\to B$ be a proper holomorphic map from a smooth complex
threefold to a smooth complex curve, with connected fibres and smooth
general fibre a complex two-torus. For every $b\in B$,
\[
                    e((Y_b)_{\rm red})\in2\Z.       \tag{C.UN.1}\label{cdp:eq:C.UN.1}
\]
This assertion allows arbitrary multiplicities and singularities and
imposes no K\"ahler or Betti-number hypothesis on $Y$.
\end{proposition}

II:8.6.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[A contracted horizontal section is elliptic]\label{cdp:st:C.2.CR.5}
In the situation of \ref{cdp:st:C.2.CR.1} one has
\[
                              g(B)=1.              \tag{C.CR.19}\label{cdp:eq:C.CR.19}
\]
In particular the remaining exact Euler expression is
\[
                            e(S)=n-v+N_0.          \tag{C.CR.20}\label{cdp:eq:C.CR.20}
\]
\end{proposition}

II:8.33.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[The contracted irrational section contributes zero]\label{cdp:st:C.2.MH.3}
In the contracted-horizontal-section situation of
\ref{cdp:st:C.2.CR.1}, the normalization resolution is ruled over
an elliptic curve and
\[
                    e(S)=0,\qquad n=v-N_0.
 \tag{C.MH.15}\label{cdp:eq:C.MH.15}
\]
\end{proposition}

II:9.2.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[Uniform multiple normal-crossings fibres]\label{cdp:st:C.2.NC.3}
Let $g:Y\to C$ be a proper holomorphic map from a connected smooth compact complex threefold with $H^2(Y,\Q)=0$ to a smooth compact curve, with connected fibres and smooth general fibre of Euler characteristic zero. A fibre $mS$ with reduced normal-crossings support has
\[
 e(S)=\tau(S)\ge0.
 \tag{C.NC.8}\label{cdp:eq:C.NC.8}
\]
The normalized base change $t=s^m$ is finite étale of degree $m$ over a neighbourhood of $S$ and has reduced normal-crossings central fibre $S'$. Its possibly disconnected components do not affect the comparisons
$e(S')=m e(S)$ and $\tau(S')=m\tau(S)$.
\end{proposition}

II:9.5.statement_prefix:
\begin{proposition}[Euler localization for torus fibrations]\label{cdp:st:C2a}
Let $g:Y\to C$ be a proper surjective holomorphic map from a connected compact complex manifold to a compact Riemann surface, with connected general fibre $F$. Let $\Sigma\subset C$ contain every critical value. Then $\Sigma$ can be chosen finite, and
\[
 e(Y)=e(C\setminus\Sigma)e(F)+\sum_{c\in\Sigma}e((Y_c)_{\rm red}). \tag{C.1}\label{cdp:eq:C.1}
\]
For a complex threefold with general fibre a complex two-torus this becomes
$\int_Yc_3(T_Y)=e(Y)=\sum_{c\in\Sigma}e((Y_c)_{\rm red})$.
\end{proposition}

